scope: |
  The owner's decision of 2026-08-15: give v3's executor a path to plugin intrinsics. It is the
  single unlock for the whole `host function '…' is not implemented on this lane` bucket — 30
  refusals today, the largest single-cause group left in the corpus.

  THE OWNER CORRECTED MY FRAMING AND HE WAS RIGHT. I had reported these as "needing prims in v2".
  They are PLUGIN INTRINSICS and they already exist: `element` in
  `v1/runtime/plugins/frontend-plugin/…/FrontendIntrinsics.scala`, `envOrElse` in `os-plugin`,
  `sha256` in the cross-backend parity list. v2 has a plugin SPI (`NativePluginHost.register`) and a
  registry (`V2PluginRegistry`, a plain name -> `List[Value] => Value` map inside `v2/src`). What v3
  has is NOTHING: grep finds the plugin SPI named in v3's charter and in six comments, and in no
  code.

  MEASURED TODAY, so the starting point is not assumed. Both lanes refuse identically and at
  LOWERING, not at run time: `the host function 'mkdirs' is not implemented on this lane` from
  `resolveExtern`'s fallback. So there is NO divergence today — there is a uniform honest refusal,
  and that is the property any fix must preserve.

  WHY THE OBVIOUS ROUTE IS WRONG, and it is recorded in `Lower.scala` because it was already tried:
  handing the extern's name straight to the lane as a prim let v2's plugins answer on the BRIDGE and
  nothing answer on the EXECUTOR, so the two lanes disagreed on 11 cases and FIVE PROGRAMS RAN AND
  PRINTED THE WRONG THING. Any design that lets one lane answer alone reproduces that.

  THE DESIGN, and the constraint that shapes it: ONE implementation must serve BOTH lanes.
    - `v3/src` gains a REGISTRY — name -> `List[Value] => Value` — mirroring `V2PluginRegistry`'s
      shape. It is kernel code with zero dependencies, which is what I-1 asks: the kernel holds the
      door, not what comes through it.
    - `Exec.prim` consults it before refusing an unknown primitive.
    - `Lower` consults it too, because the refusal is issued at LOWERING: an extern with a provider
      must lower to a `Prim` rather than to the positioned throw.
    - PROVIDERS LIVE OUTSIDE `v3/src`, in a new `v3/plugins` source directory compiled alongside —
      exactly how `alphabet/src` is already added by `v3/ssc3`. A provider registers into v3's
      registry AND into `V2PluginRegistry`, with the value conversion at that boundary, so the
      bridge answers from the SAME implementation. Both lanes agree by construction rather than by
      coincidence.

  SLICE: the design recorded in the charter, the registry, both hooks, and ONE intrinsic proven end
  to end on BOTH lanes. `mkdirs` is the candidate — JDK-only, two corpus cases, and impossible to
  get subtly wrong. The other 28 are follow-ups, not this claim.

  MEASUREMENT: N with floors DIFF 3 / CRASH 9; `mkdirs` answering identically on `ssc3 run` and
  `ssc3 run --bridge`; and the uniform-refusal property preserved for every name that still has no
  provider.
  THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER THE EXISTING PLUGINS CAN BE MADE TO WORK, AND THAT REFRAMES THE WHOLE
  CLAIM. Measured 2026-08-15, cross-referencing every failing host name against `v2/runtime/std/`:

      17 of the 19 distinct names ALREADY HAVE A v2 PLUGIN.
      `element`, `signal`, `computedSignal`, `eqSignal`, `localStorageGet`  -> ui-plugin
      `contentDocument`, `contentData`, `contentBlock`, `contentModuleMetadata` -> content-plugin
      `envOrElse`, `readLine`, `exec`                                       -> os-plugin
      `mkdirs`                                                              -> fs-plugin
      `sha256`                                                              -> actors-plugin
      `__jsonCoreWrap`, `__jsonCoreWrapStrict`                              -> json-plugin
      `add`                                                                 -> http-fast-plugin
      only `actorGroupTell` and `webauthnChallenge` have no provider at all.

  SO THE WORK IS TO LOAD THE FLEET, NOT TO WRITE PROVIDERS. That is a different and much larger
  lever: one mechanism against ~30 refusals instead of one intrinsic at a time.

  AND NEITHER LANE LOADS ANY PLUGIN TODAY — which is why the refusal is uniform and why nothing has
  drifted. `v3/ssc3` builds the bridge's v2 from `v2/src` ALONE (`classes_for ssc2 "" "$ROOT/v2/src"`)
  and the whole fleet lives in `v2/runtime/std/*-plugin/`. Discovery is
  `java.util.ServiceLoader.load(classOf[NativePlugin], …)` in `NativePluginHost`, so a plugin
  registers itself once its classes are on the classpath.

  THE BRIDGE HALF IS THEREFORE NEARLY FREE and the EXECUTOR HALF IS THE REAL WORK: an adapter
  between `ssc3.Value` and `ssc.Value`, since a v2 plugin registers `List[ssc.Value] => ssc.Value`.
  `v3/src/BridgeV2.scala` already translates v3's IR to v2's Core IR, so the value mapping is not
  starting from nothing.

  THEY MUST LAND TOGETHER, NOT IN SEQUENCE. Loading the fleet on the bridge alone changes nothing,
  because the refusal is issued at LOWERING for both lanes; and relaxing that refusal with only the
  bridge able to answer is EXACTLY the shape that once diverged 11 cases and printed five wrong
  answers. Either both lanes answer a name or neither does.
  WIDENED 2026-08-15 to `v3/src/Main.scala`, BEFORE the edit, because a wrapper `@main` does not
  work. There are TWO entry points — the kernel's `ssc3` and the uniml front's `ssc3uniml` — and
  `v3/ssc3` chooses the second whenever the front is built, which is always. A wrapper main that
  installs the fleet is therefore discarded by the uniml branch that runs after it; measured, and
  the fleet stayed uninstalled while everything else worked.
  So the install belongs where BOTH entry points already meet, `Cli.run`, and the kernel must reach
  it WITHOUT naming the provider type — by name, the way `ServiceLoader` does. That keeps I-1: a
  string is not a dependency.

  MECHANISM PROVEN SEPARATELY BEFORE THE WIRING, so the remaining failures are known to be mine:
  `NativePluginHost.loadAll()` on the fleet classpath loads 2 providers and the registry then holds
  `mkdirs`, `envOrElse`, `exec`, `exists`, `deleteFile`, `listDir`, `mkdir`, `moveFile`,
  `pathBasename` and more. The fleet works; only the install point was wrong.

  ALSO `v3/plugin-classpath.sh` — the cached fleet classpath, one module per line, the same shape
  and the same reasoning as `v3/uniml-classpath.sh`: availability is an explicit cached fact so a
  caller who is not using the fleet never waits for sbt.
next: install by name from Cli.run, prove mkdirs on both lanes, then measure
